/-!
# The inverse image of a zero scheme is the zero scheme of the inverse image section

Let `f : X ⟶ Y` be a morphism of algebraic spaces over a scheme `S` contained in a big fppf
site `B`, let `G` be an invertible `𝒪_Y`-module and let `s` be a global section of `G`.  The
zero scheme `Z(s) ⊆ Y` of Stacks 0845 and the zero scheme `Z(f^*s) ⊆ X` of the inverse image
section are both defined as intersections of closed subspaces, and this file identifies them:

  `Z(f^*s) = X ×_Y Z(s)`,

the right-hand side being the inverse image `f^{-1}(Z(s))` of Stacks 083Q, which is the fibre
product by `CategoryTheory.Subfunctor.isPullback_preimage`.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsTrivialisingChart`: an étale chart of `Y` carrying a compatible
  family of free generators of `G` over the charts above it.  This is the hypothesis under which
  the evaluation ideal of `f^*s` is computed from the evaluation ideal of `s`, and for an
  invertible `G` such charts cover `Y`.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt_preimage_zeroScheme_eq`: over a commutative diagram
  of affine étale charts whose chart of `Y` trivialises `G`, the defining ideal of
  `f^{-1}(Z(s))` is the evaluation ideal of `f^*s`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.zeroSchemeSubfunctor_modulePullback_eq_preimage`:
  **`Z(f^*s) = f^{-1}(Z(s))`.**
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.zeroScheme_modulePullback_eq_preimage`: the same as an equality of
  bundled closed subspaces.

## Implementation notes

The two containments are the two unconditional bounds on the intersection defining the zero
scheme, `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.zeroSchemeSubfunctor_le` and
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.le_zeroSchemeSubfunctor`, and both are applied to the closed subspace
`f^{-1}(Z(s))`.  Each asks for one containment between its defining ideal and the evaluation
ideal of `f^*s` on every affine étale chart of `X`, so what has to be proved is the *equality*
of those two ideals, and that equality is assembled from three inputs:

* the defining ideal of `f^{-1}(Z(s))` over an affine chart diagram is generated by the
  pullback of the defining ideal of `Z(s)`
  (`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt_preimage_eq_map_chartDiagram`);
* the defining ideal of `Z(s)` over an affine chart *is* the evaluation ideal of `s`, which is
  the surjectivity half of Stacks 03MB for the quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals of Stacks 0845
  (`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_definingIdealAt_eq_sectionEvaluationIdealAt_of_isInvertible`
  and `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt_zeroScheme_eq`);
* the evaluation ideal of `f^*s` over the chart of `X` of such a diagram is generated by the
  pullback of the evaluation ideal of `s`, as soon as the chart of `Y` trivialises `G`
  (`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionEvaluationIdealAt_modulePullback_eq_map`).

An arbitrary affine chart of `X` carries no such diagram, so the equality is proved at an
arbitrary affine chart by descent along the covering family of the charts that do: those are
cover-dense by `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.mem_topology_of_hasAffineChartDiagramWith`, whose
condition on the chart of `Y` is taken to be `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsTrivialisingChart`, and
membership in each of the two ideals is local — in the evaluation ideal by
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.mem_sectionEvaluationIdealAt_of_sieve`, in the defining ideal by
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.mem_definingIdealAt_of_sieve`.  The first of the two needs its
hypothesis only at the affine members of the covering sieve, which is what makes the sieve
generated by the good charts usable for it; for the second the sieve is taken to be the
membership sieve itself, which is a sieve because the defining ideals are stable under
restriction.

